      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We first give the definition of the volume of the sub-level set for cost J.",
      "bbox": [
        107.53201293945312,
        297.0900573730469,
        392.5276794433594,
        307.05267333984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Definition 1. Let F ∶Rd →R be a measurable function. Define the volume of the sub-level set for a",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        308.6639099121094,
        504.0003967285156,
        322.9346618652344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "given level t as:",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        323.88104248046875,
        171.4822998046875,
        333.8436584472656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "VF (t) = ∫Rd χ{Y ∈Rd∶F (Y )≤t}(Y )dY,",
      "bbox": [
        230.26400756835938,
        341.1258850097656,
        381.7472229003906,
        357.3438720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where χ denotes the indicator function.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        363.47601318359375,
        264.9579162597656,
        373.4386291503906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The volume function VJ(t) plays a crucial role in linking geometric properties with probabilistic",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        384.33990478515625,
        504.00152587890625,
        395.54742431640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "outcomes in optimization and learning algorithms. This function provides a quantitative measure that",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        396.46435546875,
        503.9952087402344,
        405.37091064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "helps us to understand how changes in parameters like λ influence the distribution and concentration",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        406.6560363769531,
        504.00042724609375,
        416.61865234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of probability mass.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        418.2823486328125,
        187.42184448242188,
        427.18890380859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The interplay between geometry and probability, represented by VJ(t), is crucial for evaluating the",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        433.45489501953125,
        503.994873046875,
        444.66241455078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "convergence and stability of algorithms. It provides a significant method for utilizing the PDF of the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        445.5793762207031,
        504.0034484863281,
        454.4859313964844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "random variable Y to constrain the CDF, thereby facilitating convergence in distribution.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        456.3299560546875,
        462.6897888183594,
        465.3959045410156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Proposition 2. Given the target distribution Y ∼p(⋅) with P(Y ) ∝exp(−J(Y )",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        471.9686279296875,
        432.5771179199219,
        484.6226501464844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "λ",
      "bbox": [
        421.0509948730469,
        480.5106506347656,
        425.77923583984375,
        487.48443603515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "),Y ∈Rd, where",
      "bbox": [
        433.7720031738281,
        473.0916442871094,
        503.99542236328125,
        484.6226501464844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "J is a cost function with minY J(Y ) = 0 and Y ∗= arg minJ(Y ), and assuming that the volume",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        488.35589599609375,
        504.0001220703125,
        500.3106384277344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "function VJ(t) is bounded by polynomial inequalities:",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
